	<p style="text-align: justify;" class="indent">&nbsp; &nbsp;When the British arrived towards the beginning of the age of Industrial Revolution, it was one of the handful original hindu temples to have survived in Punjab after the onslought of centuries of 'at times' grotesque Jihad. During the Independance of India in 1947, this last association was also taken away, as our homes were invaded and at the risk of being butchered alive or worse (Okoth-Obbo, 2020, August, 2), my great grandfather Chief Engineer at Royal Multan Electricity Board 'Late Moolchand Sarna, son of Pyare Lal Sarna' by God's immense grace and due to childhood muslim friend's last minute tip, barely escaped with his life at the last minute across the border to "Modern India". It is the Śrīmad-Bhāgavatam text's Cantoh seven on five year old His [Her/Their] Holiness Devotee Prince Prahalad (Prabhupāda, 1970s), which is now our last archiological proof and connection to our revered tangata/people whenua/land. Happy Mother's Day to My <a href="http://kumari-raj-sarna-scholarship.azurewebsites.net/">Late Parental Grandmother</a>, the last surviving member of my whanau/family born in Multan, who passed away in 2024.</p>	
